{
 "id": "l08",
 "label": "8",
 "title": "Possession: ng, ni and my, your, his",
 "summary": "How to say whose something is, with names, nouns and pronouns",
 "minutes": 35,
 "goals": [
  "Use ni, nina, ng and ng mga to show an owner",
  "Use the possessive pronouns before a noun (aking) and after a noun (ko)",
  "Avoid echoing the same possessive form twice in one sentence",
  "Place possessives correctly with plural nouns",
  "Use nito, niyan and niyon for the owner of something"
 ],
 "sections": [
  {
   "title": "Owners: ng and ni",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "To show **whose** something is, Tagalog puts the **thing first** and the **owner second**, with a marker between them. English does it the other way round (\"Ana's house\"), so the order feels backwards at first."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Owner is…",
      "Marker",
      "Example",
      "Meaning"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "one person's name",
       "[[ni|marker]]",
       "ang bahay **ni** Ana",
       "Ana's house"
      ],
      [
       "two or more names",
       "[[nina|marker]]",
       "ang bahay **nina** Ana at Ben",
       "Ana and Ben's house"
      ],
      [
       "a common noun (one)",
       "[[ng|marker]]",
       "ang damit **ng** bata",
       "the child's dress"
      ],
      [
       "a common noun (more than one)",
       "[[ng mga|marker]]",
       "ang damit **ng mga** bata",
       "the children's clothes"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Owner markers"
    },
