  {
   "title": "My, your, his: possessive pronouns",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "There are seven possessive pronouns. Each has **two forms**. One goes **before** the noun (aking bahay) and the other goes **after** the noun (bahay ko). They mean the same."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "English",
      "Before the noun",
      "After the noun"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "my",
       "[[aking|poss]]",
       "[[ko|poss]]"
      ],
      [
       "your (one person)",
       "[[iyong|poss]]",
       "[[mo|poss]]"
      ],
      [
       "his, her",
       "[[kaniyang|poss]]  (or [[kanyang|poss]])",
       "[[niya|poss]]"
      ],
      [
       "our (not including you)",
       "[[aming|poss]]",
       "[[namin|poss]]"
      ],
      [
       "our (including you)",
       "[[ating|poss]]",
       "[[natin|poss]]"
      ],
      [
       "your (plural or polite)",
       "[[inyong|poss]]",
       "[[ninyo|poss]]"
      ],
      [
       "their",
       "[[kanilang|poss]]",
       "[[nila|poss]]"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Two forms of every possessive"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "english",
     "title": "Same two kinds of we",
     "t": "Just as in Lesson 6, there are two kinds of \"our\". **Aming** and **namin** leave the listener out. **Ating** and **natin** include the listener."
    },
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "Before the noun: a linker is needed"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "The before-noun forms are really the basic forms **akin, iyo, kaniya, amin, atin, inyo, kanila** with a linker attached, which is why they end in -ng. So **akin** + g = **aking**."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
