  {
   "title": "Do not echo the same form twice",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Sometimes one sentence has two possessions of the same person: \"my mother\" and \"my house\". Tagalog prefers to use **one before-form and one after-form** so the sounds do not repeat."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Sentence",
      "Verdict"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "Ang **aking** ina ay pumunta sa bahay **ko**.",
       "Good: one before-form, one after-form"
      ],
      [
       "Ang ina **ko** ay pumunta sa **aking** bahay.",
       "Good: the other way round"
      ],
      [
       "Ang **aking** ina ay pumunta sa **aking** bahay.",
       "Not good: aking is repeated"
      ],
      [
       "Ang ina **ko** ay pumunta sa bahay **ko**.",
       "Not good: ko is repeated"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Mixing the two styles"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "(\"Ang aking ina ay pumunta sa bahay ko\" means \"My mother went to my house.\")"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "tip",
     "title": "Sound check",
     "t": "Say both versions aloud. The mixed version sounds smoother, and you will quickly hear the repetition in the others."
